 talk about because my viewers are  interested they may also be interested  in golf but they're primarily interested  in learning languages  and i was thinking as we were going  through this that you're a coach yes  you're a motivator you're a teacher  and i'm sure there are things in common  between motivating and coaching people  when it comes to learning languages and  when it comes to learning to play golf  better so from your perspective what do  you try to achieve as a coach i try to  you know take people and show them  what's possible you know i mean i think  of in languages and i think man i'd sure  love to learn another language and for  me you know if i was to kind of  say if i was a language coach how would  i inspire people to learn a language i  know what i do  i'd say my goal is to go visit such and  such country france or spain and i can't  wait to go play a golf journey there for  seven days and it might be a year 18  months away and then i think okay i want  to play golf in spain well i want to  learn how to speak the language too so i  would i would set a goal for myself that  i think oh i love playing golf and i  love playing golf in spain and i know i  could get around fine probably in spain  with my english but i would want to  learn the language so i would pick  something that i love which is golf and  then i would do something that i would  aspire to be better at which is a second  language that's how kind of i would do  it okay you know now as it relates to  you you asked me the question i kind of  wanted a tangent right the  you know how i inspire people to play  better golf as i  you know everybody wants the quick fix  they want the pixie dust they want they  just want it now that's the attitude we  have and as a coach i try to inspire  people to understand that it's subtle  skills  that we stack upon one another and we  can build those subtle skills we become  much better much more educated and then  as we're conscious we tend to put we can  automate things you have to learn the  basics in golf you have to learn a few  different things about club face path  how the ball reacts and that's really  stuff you can't there's no shortcuts to  that i'm sure when you're teaching  languages there's no shortcuts to  certain things too and it's not simply a  matter of explaining those things it's a  matter of grooving them right through  the various drills that you showed us  right uh and i think in language  learning too i i have the same approach  i feel that anyone can learn and i think  you encouraged us here we can all become  better right some will be better than  others there are some differences but we  can all improve uh but we have to put  the effort in it's not as you say it's  not pixie dust language learning as well  you know it takes time i always say in  language learning there's three issues  there's attitude yeah whether you like  the language whether you're you know  willing to put the effort in whether  you're fighting the language so attitude  sure the second thing is time yeah and  the third thing is developing that  ability to notice what is happening in  the language you're learning right but  if i refer to if i apply that to golf  works obviously if you don't like the  game of golf if you're not willing to  put in the effort right if you don't put  in the time and if you don't notice the  things that you mentioned for example  how the face of the club hits the ball  right you're gonna have a tough time  improving so there are similarities  that's a great parallel yeah yeah and so  you know and and are you consider  yourself a teacher or a coach  i like to i like to think i'm a conduit  to  you know to somebody learning something  right i mean you know it's hard to teach  golf you know you have to provide  different experiences that somebody goes  oh and they have these subtle little aha  moments and eventually you have a series  of aha moments and then you feel like  you have a certain co